def two_sum(nums, target):
    num_to_index = {}
    for i, num in enumerate(nums):
        complement = target - num
        if complement in num_to_index:
            return [num_to_index[complement], i]
        num_to_index[num] = i
    return None
```

This solution uses a hash map (`num_to_index`) to store each number and its index as you iterate through the array. For each number, it calculates the complement (the number needed to reach the target) and checks if the complement is already in the hash map. If it is, it returns the indices of the current number and the complement. This ensures the solution runs in O(n) time. Later, revisit hash maps and their time complexity to solidify your understanding.

